00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:03.590 align:start position:0%
 
so our next speaker besides being a  fan of stan which we clearly like at  this conference  she also loves loops  she loves loops in r which some of us  might  be scared by  but if you do them right they're not  necessarily slower they have a bad rep  but if you do them right they're not  necessarily bad  but she's also a fan of loops in music  and she even has  a looper to put her voice over her  recordings and the looper's name is  lupita  so  everyone please welcome to the stage  miyari  hi um my name is majeri and i also go by  may  and i'll be talking about my experience  handling the missing data of a  randomized control trial with syrian  refugee children using tools in r  so  first i'm going to give you just a  little bit of context about our study  and the challenges that it presented us  then i'll do an overview of the method  that i use  um  and i'll explain some of the tools and  strategies that were helpful to navigate  the main challenges  and then i'll illustrate the  implications of missing data on some of  our results  the international rescue committee and  nyu global ties for children have  conducted several trials to test the  impact of a program for displaced  children in conflict-affected regions  and the program basically offered  support for their academic and their  emotional development  and  one of those trials was conducted in  lebanon with syrian refugee kids which  is the data that i will be telling you a  little bit more about today  the study relied on survey data and it  involved different assessments that  covered multiple aspects of the  children's circumstances and development  and there were three waves of data  collection  the goal was that this data could cater  to multiple research questions and  analysis that would contribute to our  understanding of these programs and of  these children's development in a  comprehensive way  but these populations are fairly  different from those that have been used  in the past to generate most of the  evidence base that we have about child  development in the western world  and so  the education and child development  experts at nyu aim to cover  all possible ground in terms of  collecting background information  it's also worth noting that this is a  context where rigorous data collection  is very difficult to implement  due to security threats  due to the children's migration patterns  and also due to the lack of id  documentation for tracking them  so  we ended up with over 900 potentially  relevant items to account for  of which over 800 had missing data  and one of the first challenges is  how do we leverage all of that  potentially relevant information in a  context where we know so little and for  which we may have culturally inadequate  biases  and so we want to have a way  to also reflect in our final analysis  how cautious we should be about our  results due to the missing data  and data quality also varied across  different features due to volatile  circumstances in the field  and on top of that we had items at the  site level at the teacher level and at  the student level  as well as a bunch of items that  depended on skip logic or had weird  applicability dependencies  all things that can make addressing the  missing data a little bit less  straightforward  but  why is missing data even a problem  when we have missing data for some  participants there is always a very good  chance that their data is missing  because of important differences between  people  which can cause people that face  specific circumstances to be under or  overrepresented in our results so  basically the results risk being biased  but also  data is usually costly to collect right  and if you leave out the incomplete  cases from your study you'll be throwing  away very valuable information  and if we're going to burden vulnerable  populations with lengthy surveys  on top of the financial cost we also  have a moral responsibility to make the  best possible use of their data  um okay so  to make sure that everyone has a sense  of what's going on moving forward i'll  do a quick overview of the method  that i use to address the missing data  and first  if this is a new word for you imputation  basically means creating predictions or  best guesses for the missing information  there are many ways to go about imputing  and my choice was multiple imputation  through chained equations or chained  models i think chain models makes more  sense  this approach basically allows us to use  all the available information from each  participant to build a predictive model  for each variable that has missing data  and to create an educated guess that is  powered by your data  of what the unobserved responses might  look like  so for a second let's just pretend that  we have a super simple data set  with only a few variables  and the red cells indicate missing  information  so we start by  filling in random starting points in  place of the missing values  and then build a model for any one  variable say income  and this replaces our missing values in  income with something that is better  than just noise  and  then we can use all the available  information and our income variable with  imputations that are already better than  noise to build a predictive model for  another variable  and now instead of missing values we  have better than noise predictions for  two of our variables and we can build a  model to get even better predictions for  a third item with missing data  and so we get to a point where  we have educated guesses for all the  missing data powered partially by noise  but partially by all the observed  information from our data set  and we can keep on improving these  predictions by iterating this process  using better and better predicted values  in place of the missing data and  that is basically what we refer to as  chained models or chained equations  so  where does the  multiple part in multiple invitation  come from  it comes from repeating this full  process  multiple times but starting with  different noisy values that could lead  us to end  in slightly different places  this results in  us getting  many different completed data sets with  different final predictions for the  missing values  and i think it helps to think about them  as all the possible universes that we  can imagine  given what we were able to observe  but  let's go back to our real world data and  challenges  if we're using the mice approach to deal  with the missing data and we want to  stay humble on what we don't know about  a new context  then we may want to allow for the  possibility that any of the features  that our experts thought to collect data  on could play a role in predicting any  one variable or in explaining why some  of its information is missing  but this implies building very costly  models  that account for tons of features  and then we'd have to fit those costly  models for the hundreds of variables  that have missing data  and then because  missingness rates in data quality  variety across items  you may also want to iterate for as long  as it takes the last variable to  converge  and also you may want to make sure that  you produced enough imputed sets to get  a good estimation of the uncertainty for  all the variables that will be used for  any analysis  but all of this is super computationally  costly and time consuming to run  so  one of the strategies that i found  helpful to reduce time and resources  with no apparent loss in the quality of  the imputations was to  simply do a little bit of feature  selection before computing  specifically i found the boruta package  helpful  this is a wrapper of the random forest  method which most of you are probably  familiar with already if you're not just  know that random forest is a machine  learning algorithm that you can use to  build predictive models without having  to make  a bunch of assumptions about the  functional form that relates the  predictors with the outcome  it also rates the predictor's  importance  and  the boruta package uses the importance  scores produced by random forest  not only to rank the features but also  to help estimate which ones don't really  add any predictive value  the general idea is that it creates fake  copies of all the variables but with  their values all shuffled so that the  fake copies are just noise features with  no true predictive value  then it lets random forest score the  importance of both the real and the fake  features  and by comparing the important scores of  the real variables with those that we  know to be purely noise  it gives us suggestions of which  variables should be accepted or rejected  as having any predictive value  but  there is a catch  like most feature selection tools out  there  you need to feed it complete data  which at this point you still don't have  because that's exactly what we are  trying to address  um  so  the practical get around that i used for  this was  just to fill in the missing entries with  noise  then  run boruta with each variable that had  missing data as the outcome  and also  run boruta with a non-response  indicators as the outcome  so we can take into account the features  that are related to the probability of  missing that information  and  then i just took a conservative approach  when discarding predictors i flagged for  any one variable  only those that came back as completely  useless both to predict the variable  itself and the probability that it's  missing  here  for example you can see  which of the features on the x-axis were  found to have no predictive value for  the items of a math assessment  green indicates the count of math items  that a feature had predicted value for  and so you can already see how by  leaving all these uninformative features  out from each model iteration of the  math items you'll save resources  the software tools that i found most  helpful to implement multiple imputation  were the well-known mice package  and mice ads which complements a lot of  the functionality of mice  some of the appeal of these packages is  that they can handle mixed types of data  and multi-level imputation which allows  us to get predictions that are  consistent when we had missingness at  both the teacher and the student levels  um they also allow for a good amount of  flexibility and control over what goes  into the models  our data also had a bunch of  applicability restrictions and skip  logic patterns  at both the teacher and the student  levels and the flexibility of these  tools helped us prevent  predictions from happening where they  shouldn't be happening  also  these packages offer several options  when it comes to choosing the types of  models that you want to fit in each  iteration of mice  i decided to go with random forest  because it's significantly faster than  linear models  it naturally accounts for interactions  and non-linearities  it prevents us from making too many  assumptions about the data generating  process in a culturally different  setting where we know very little  and it is also consistent with the  feature selection process which is nice  one of the ways  in which mice offers flexibility is the  control that it gives you over the  predictor matrix  there you can make a bunch of  specifications of what goes into each  model and which variables are at what  level of nesting  and before getting started you can  automatically use the flags from your  feature selection process to populate  the predictor matrix  and you can exclude a bunch of features  that are unhelpful for each variable  that will be imputed  it was also helpful to create objects  that indicate applicability dependencies  i use those to  automatically create interaction terms  and populate the predictor matrix  and this ensured that  all the applicable items for each  student  could be used to power the imputation  models  even when applicability varied across  students  another object  that helps complement this functionality  is the where matrix  which allows to specify areas of the  sample for which invitations are not  applicable  and then there are the objects that  allow you to specify how to model each  variable  the math vector  is where you should specify the method  that should be used for the lower level  items or the student level items in our  case or where you specify  which variable is a level two item or a  teacher level item  or you can also specify  if a variable should be passively  imputed and then the imputation function  list should contain the modeling  approach that you want for only the  teacher level or level two variables  and lastly for those teacher variables  the clustering id needs to be specified  as a list in the cluster bar object  and  once you have carefully populated all  those objects you can run mice and get a  bunch of the parallel universes that are  possible for your data  we produced 100 to be safe  i'm not going to dive into this but just  know that  after you're done computing you would  have to make sure that your imputations  are converging and blending properly  and  you may want to check that there are no  obvious lingering differences between  respondents and non-respondents  once you have conditioned on all the  relevant features  and then you'll be ready to  do your analysis and get a better sense  of  the role that missing data played in the  conclusions that you can draw from it  here i'm showing you the estimated  average treatment effect  on just four of our outcomes after we  pulled the results we got from the  different imputed data sets  where here our credible intervals  already reflect the uncertainty brought  in by the missing data  and in the plots below you can see how  much the results varied across different  predicted worlds  and  you can see that for some of our  outcomes the results were pretty  consistent across imputed sets  while for other outcomes the results  varied importantly  meaning that  we can't have as much certainty about  the impact of the program on those two  outcomes because of the missing data  and so we should be more careful when  making recommendations about those  outcomes  so  just some takeaways if you are  dealing with social data  missing data is almost inevitable  and it gets worse with vulnerable  populations  so  i think the responsible thing to do is  to make an effort to assess the damage  that missing data can have on your  ability to answer your original research  questions  and multiple computation provides a nice  framework to do that  mice mice ads boruta and random forest  packages in r can help you navigate some  of the challenges if you're dealing with  complex social data  and  it is important to try to be aware and  transparent about what you can and what  you cannot learn from your data  if anyone has questions for me or if you  just want someone to vent with about  your own missing data misadventures  feel free to reach me at  mmd486 at  nyu.edu  thank you  thank you very much for that talk uh it  was wonderful um  and that's an example of finding someone  at one of our events and then he had to  become a speaker  i think that is a wonderful thing when  that happens  that was a great talk i hope everyone  enjoyed that

